---
import Section from './Section.astro';

/*
  The positioning statement.

  First thing after the hero, and the sentence that has to do the most work on
  the whole site: it's what a hiring manager or a client reads before deciding
  whether to keep scrolling.

  PHASE 0: this copy is my best guess at Brendon's positioning, not his own
  words. It's here so the section can be built and judged — replace it. The
  shape to keep is a plain claim about outcomes, not a list of technologies.

  The headline is split per character by the motion module. It carries an
  aria-label automatically, so screen readers get the sentence rather than
  thirty-one separate letters.
*/
---

<Section surface="base" width="content" revealGroup>
  <p class="font-body text-2xs tracking-widest text-fg-faint uppercase" data-reveal>
    What I actually do
  </p>

  <h2
    class="mt-8 max-w-[18ch] font-display text-5xl leading-[0.92] uppercase md:text-7xl"
    style="letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display);"
    data-split
  >
    Websites that hold up after launch.
  </h2>

  <div class="mt-12 grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-2">
    <p class="max-w-measure font-body text-lg text-fg md:text-xl" data-reveal>
      Most sites I inherit were built to look finished on launch day and not much further.
      I build the other kind — fast, accessible, and structured so the next change doesn't
      break the last one.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-measure font-body text-base text-fg-muted" data-reveal>
      I work mostly with marketing teams and small businesses, which means the brief usually
      arrives as a number rather than a page. That suits me: it's easier to tell whether the
      work succeeded.
    </p>
  </div>
</Section>
